---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import { GoldMineSoundExporter } from "../../components/cave/greedy-gold-miner/GoldMineSoundExporter";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="How to Escape With the Most Gold"
  description="Find the richest escape route: the path that collects the most gold before reaching the exit"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <GoldMineSoundExporter client:only="react" />
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        You enter a gold mine with one mission: become filthy rich and make it
        out alive. But there's a catch. Every step you take collects gold... and
        collapses the tunnel behind you. There is no going back.
      </p><p>
        Luckily, there is another way out. So which route leaves you the richest
        by the time you escape?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <pre>
      MINE_MAP = [
        "###############",
        "#.#.....#.....#",
        "#.###.#.###.#.#",
        "#.....#...#.#.#",
        "####S####.#.#.#",
        "#.......#.#.#.#",
        "#.#.#.#.#...###",
        "#.#.#.#...#...#",
        "#.#.#.###.#.#.#",
        "#.#.#.......#.#",
        "###.###.#.###.#",
        "#.....#.#.....#",
        "#.#####.###.#.#",
        "#.....#...#...#",
        "#.###.###.#.#.#",
        "#...#.....#.#.#",
        "###.#.#####.###",
        "#.#...#....E..#",
        "#.###.#######.#",
        "#.............#",
        "###############",
      ]
    </pre>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        If we use BFS, we get the shortest path. But that is not what we want.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        If we use DFS, we get one valid path. But not necessarily the one with
        the most gold.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        What should we do? We can slightly modify DFS to explore all possible
        paths. This is DFS with backtracking.
      </p>
      <p>
        When we find a path, we don't stop. We go back and try every other
        option. Each time we find a path, we count the gold and keep the best
        one so far.
      </p><p>
        This is hard because we must explore all paths. And believe it or not,
        this becomes infeasible very quickly, even for small grids. For example,
        this grid already has 106 possible paths.
      </p>
      <p>But how we can optimize this is a story for another video.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <!-- Full script:

  You enter a gold mine with one mission: become filthy rich and make it out alive.

  But there's a catch.

  Every step you take collects gold… and collapses the tunnel behind you.

  There is no going back.

  Luckily, there is another way out.

  So which route leaves you the richest by the time you escape?

  If we use BFS, we get the shortest path.

  But that is not what we want.

  If we use DFS, we get one valid path.

  But not necessarily the one with the most gold.

  What should we do?

  We can slightly modify DFS to explore all possible paths.

  This is DFS with backtracking.

  When we find a path, we don't stop.

  We go back and try every other option.

  Each time we find a path, we count the gold and keep the best one so far.

  This is hard because we must explore all paths.

  And believe it or not, this becomes infeasible very quickly, even for small grids.

  For example, this grid already has 106 possible paths.

  But how to optimize this… that's a story for another video.
  
  -->
</RevealLayout>
